{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-33525" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}}⟨1,t⟩.\n$$\n\nFind the principal normal vector at the point where $t=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068383/state.json" "file_sha256" "dca4d73da748321ea9be4275fec60a4462e60b0acef5a844f0115020b2c0aef3"} {"question" "q-33525" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}}⟨1,t⟩) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}})⟨1,t⟩+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}}⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)⟩ \\\\  & =-\\frac{t}{(1+{t}^{2})^{3/2}}⟨1,t⟩+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}}⟨0,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(1+{t}^{2})^{3/2}}[-t⟨1,t⟩+(1+{t}^{2})⟨0,1⟩] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(1+{t}^{2})^{3/2}}⟨-t,1⟩ \\\\  \\\\ {T}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{(1+{1}^{2})^{3/2}}⟨-1,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}⟨-1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(1)‖ & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}‖⟨-1,1⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(1)$ and obtain the principal normal vector to the curve at the point where $t=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(1) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(1)}{‖{T}^{′}(1)‖}=\\frac{\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}⟨-1,1⟩}{\\frac{1}{2}}=⟨-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that when we find the scalar product of $T$ and $N$ at the point where $t=1,$  we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(1)\\cdot N(1) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,1⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068383/state.json" "file_sha256" "dca4d73da748321ea9be4275fec60a4462e60b0acef5a844f0115020b2c0aef3"} {"question" "q-33525" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068383/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "54539cf7dfb885572033a2bf0861ba4715d20e2968cedb79235051206f0c8ae8"} {"question" "q-33525" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068383/state.json" "file_sha256" "dca4d73da748321ea9be4275fec60a4462e60b0acef5a844f0115020b2c0aef3"} {"question" "q-33525" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "⟨-1,0⟩"] ["math" "⟨-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟩"] ["math" "⟨0,1⟩"] ["math" "⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟩"] ["math" "⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟩"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068383/state.json" "file_sha256" "dca4d73da748321ea9be4275fec60a4462e60b0acef5a844f0115020b2c0aef3"} {"question" "q-33525" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "⟨-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟩"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068383/state.json" "file_sha256" "dca4d73da748321ea9be4275fec60a4462e60b0acef5a844f0115020b2c0aef3"} {"question" "q-33233" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The equations of a curve $r(t)$ and its corresponding unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at an arbitrary point are given below. Find the principal normal vector to that curve at the point $P(0,3,4).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ) \\\\ T(t) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,k,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi )\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068383/state.json" "file_sha256" "dca4d73da748321ea9be4275fec60a4462e60b0acef5a844f0115020b2c0aef3"} {"question" "q-33233" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to the point $P(0,3,4)$ on the given curve. We know that:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨0,3,4⟩ \\\\ 5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k & =0\\text{ }i+3\\text{ }j+4\\text{ }k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the components of $i$, $j$, and $k$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=0 \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=3 \\\\ 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=4\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equality, $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ This value also satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, $P(0,3,4)$ is at the tip of the vector $r(\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$ \n\nNow, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,k \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j-\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k \\\\ {T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,i-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,j-\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{5}\\,j-\\frac{4}{5}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})‖ & =\\sqrt{(0)^{2}+{(-\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}+{(-\\frac{4}{5})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{25}+\\frac{16}{25}} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve $r(t)$ at the point $P(0,3,4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{‖{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})‖}=\\frac{-\\frac{3}{5}\\,j-\\frac{4}{5}\\,k}{1}=-\\frac{3}{5}\\,j-\\frac{4}{5}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that when we find the scalar product of $T$ and $N$ at the point where at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot N(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =(-i)\\cdot (-\\frac{3}{5}\\,j-\\frac{4}{5}\\,k) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}(i\\cdot j)+\\frac{4}{5}(i\\cdot k) \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068383/state.json" "file_sha256" "dca4d73da748321ea9be4275fec60a4462e60b0acef5a844f0115020b2c0aef3"} {"question" "q-33233" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068383/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "54539cf7dfb885572033a2bf0861ba4715d20e2968cedb79235051206f0c8ae8"} {"question" "q-33233" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068383/state.json" "file_sha256" "dca4d73da748321ea9be4275fec60a4462e60b0acef5a844f0115020b2c0aef3"} {"question" "q-33233" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{3}{5}\\,j-\\frac{4}{5}\\,k"] ["math" "-\\frac{4}{5}\\,i-\\frac{3}{5}\\,j"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{5}\\,i+\\frac{4}{5}\\,j"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{5}\\,i-\\frac{4}{5}\\,k"] ["math" "\\frac{4}{5}\\,j-\\frac{3}{5}\\,k"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068383/state.json" "file_sha256" "dca4d73da748321ea9be4275fec60a4462e60b0acef5a844f0115020b2c0aef3"} {"question" "q-33233" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{3}{5}\\,j-\\frac{4}{5}\\,k"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068383/state.json" "file_sha256" "dca4d73da748321ea9be4275fec60a4462e60b0acef5a844f0115020b2c0aef3"} {"question" "q-74851" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+16}}\\cdot ⟨t,4⟩.\n$$\n\nFind the principal normal vector at the point where $t=0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068383/state.json" "file_sha256" "dca4d73da748321ea9be4275fec60a4462e60b0acef5a844f0115020b2c0aef3"} {"question" "q-74851" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+16}}\\cdot ⟨t,4⟩) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+16}})\\cdot ⟨t,4⟩+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+16}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(⟨t,4⟩) \\\\  & =-\\frac{t}{({t}^{2}+16)\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+16}}\\cdot ⟨t,4⟩+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+16}}\\cdot ⟨1,0⟩ \\\\  \\\\ {T}^{′}(0) & =-\\frac{0}{({0}^{2}+16)\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+16}}\\cdot ⟨0,4⟩+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+16}}\\cdot ⟨1,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}⟨1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(0)‖ & =\\frac{1}{4}‖⟨1,0⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(0)$ and obtain the principal normal vector to the curve at the point where $t=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(0) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(0)}{‖{T}^{′}(0)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{4}⟨1,0⟩}{(\\frac{1}{4})} \\\\  & =⟨1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that when we find the scalar product of $T$ and $N$ at the point where $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(0)\\cdot N(0) & =\\frac{1}{4}⟨0,4⟩\\cdot ⟨1,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(0+0) \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068383/state.json" "file_sha256" "dca4d73da748321ea9be4275fec60a4462e60b0acef5a844f0115020b2c0aef3"} {"question" "q-74851" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068383/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "54539cf7dfb885572033a2bf0861ba4715d20e2968cedb79235051206f0c8ae8"} {"question" "q-74851" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068383/state.json" "file_sha256" "dca4d73da748321ea9be4275fec60a4462e60b0acef5a844f0115020b2c0aef3"} {"question" "q-74851" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨-1,1⟩"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨1,-1⟩"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨1,1⟩"] ["math" "⟨-1,0⟩"] ["math" "⟨1,0⟩"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068383/state.json" "file_sha256" "dca4d73da748321ea9be4275fec60a4462e60b0acef5a844f0115020b2c0aef3"} {"question" "q-74851" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "⟨1,0⟩"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068383/state.json" "file_sha256" "dca4d73da748321ea9be4275fec60a4462e60b0acef5a844f0115020b2c0aef3"} {"question" "q-127333" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}}⟨1,\\,2t⟩.\n$$\n\nFind the principal normal vector at the point where $t=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068383/state.json" "file_sha256" "dca4d73da748321ea9be4275fec60a4462e60b0acef5a844f0115020b2c0aef3"} {"question" "q-127333" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}}⟨1,\\,2t⟩) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}})⟨1,\\,2t⟩+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}}⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)⟩ \\\\  & =-\\frac{4t}{(4{t}^{2}+1)^{3/2}}⟨1,\\,2t⟩+\\frac{1}{(4{t}^{2}+1)^{1/2}}⟨0,\\,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(4{t}^{2}+1)^{3/2}}[-4t⟨1,\\,2t⟩+(4{t}^{2}+1)⟨0,\\,2⟩] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(4{t}^{2}+1)^{3/2}}⟨-4t,\\,2\\,⟩ \\\\ {T}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{(4(1)^{2}+1)^{3/2}}⟨-4(1),\\,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{5}^{3}}}⟨-4,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5\\sqrt{5}}⟨-2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(1)‖ & =\\frac{2}{5\\sqrt{5}}‖⟨-2,1⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5\\sqrt{5}}\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+(1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5\\sqrt{5}}\\sqrt{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(1)$ and obtain the principal normal vector to the curve at the point where $t=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(1) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(1)}{‖{T}^{′}(1)‖}=\\frac{\\frac{2}{5\\sqrt{5}}⟨-2,1⟩}{(\\frac{2}{5})}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨-2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that when we find the scalar product of $N$ and $T$ at the point where $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(1)\\cdot T(1) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨-2,1⟩\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨1,\\,2⟩) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(⟨-2,1⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,2⟩) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}((-2)(1)+(1)(2)) \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068383/state.json" "file_sha256" "dca4d73da748321ea9be4275fec60a4462e60b0acef5a844f0115020b2c0aef3"} {"question" "q-127333" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068383/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "54539cf7dfb885572033a2bf0861ba4715d20e2968cedb79235051206f0c8ae8"} {"question" "q-127333" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068383/state.json" "file_sha256" "dca4d73da748321ea9be4275fec60a4462e60b0acef5a844f0115020b2c0aef3"} {"question" "q-127333" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-1,1⟩"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,1⟩"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨-2,1⟩"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨2,1⟩"] ["math" "⟨-1,0⟩"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068383/state.json" "file_sha256" "dca4d73da748321ea9be4275fec60a4462e60b0acef5a844f0115020b2c0aef3"} {"question" "q-127333" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨-2,1⟩"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068383/state.json" "file_sha256" "dca4d73da748321ea9be4275fec60a4462e60b0acef5a844f0115020b2c0aef3"} {"question" "q-127335" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩.\n$$\n\nFind the principal normal vector at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068383/state.json" "file_sha256" "dca4d73da748321ea9be4275fec60a4462e60b0acef5a844f0115020b2c0aef3"} {"question" "q-127335" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩) \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t⟩ \\\\ {T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =⟨-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}),\\,-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,-\\sqrt{3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})‖ & =‖⟨-1,-\\sqrt{3}⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(\\sqrt{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})$ and obtain the principal normal vector to the curve at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})}{‖{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨-1,-\\sqrt{3}⟩}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨-1,-\\sqrt{3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that when we find the scalar product of $N$ and $T$ at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\cdot N(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =⟨-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{1}{2}⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}⟨-1,-\\sqrt{3}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}((-\\sqrt{3})(-1)+(1)(-\\sqrt{3})) \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068383/state.json" "file_sha256" "dca4d73da748321ea9be4275fec60a4462e60b0acef5a844f0115020b2c0aef3"} {"question" "q-127335" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068383/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "54539cf7dfb885572033a2bf0861ba4715d20e2968cedb79235051206f0c8ae8"} {"question" "q-127335" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068383/state.json" "file_sha256" "dca4d73da748321ea9be4275fec60a4462e60b0acef5a844f0115020b2c0aef3"} {"question" "q-127335" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{1}{2}⟨-1,-\\sqrt{3}⟩"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}⟨1,\\sqrt{3}⟩"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}⟨-1,-3⟩"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}⟨-\\sqrt{3},1⟩"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨2,-1⟩"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068383/state.json" "file_sha256" "dca4d73da748321ea9be4275fec60a4462e60b0acef5a844f0115020b2c0aef3"} {"question" "q-127335" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{2}⟨-1,-\\sqrt{3}⟩"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068383/state.json" "file_sha256" "dca4d73da748321ea9be4275fec60a4462e60b0acef5a844f0115020b2c0aef3"} {"question" "q-223102" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nT(t)=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.\n$$\n\nThe unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given above. Find the principal normal vector to that curve at an arbitrary point on the curve.\n\n$N(t)=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$\n$⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,$\n{{field-1}}\n$⟩$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068383/state.json" "file_sha256" "dca4d73da748321ea9be4275fec60a4462e60b0acef5a844f0115020b2c0aef3"} {"question" "q-223102" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =|-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}|‖⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\sqrt{2({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩}{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068383/state.json" "file_sha256" "dca4d73da748321ea9be4275fec60a4462e60b0acef5a844f0115020b2c0aef3"} {"question" "q-223102" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068383/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "54539cf7dfb885572033a2bf0861ba4715d20e2968cedb79235051206f0c8ae8"} {"question" "q-223102" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068383/state.json" "file_sha256" "dca4d73da748321ea9be4275fec60a4462e60b0acef5a844f0115020b2c0aef3"} {"question" "q-223102" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sin(t)-\\cos(t)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068383/state.json" "file_sha256" "dca4d73da748321ea9be4275fec60a4462e60b0acef5a844f0115020b2c0aef3"} {"question" "q-358782" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,12t⟩$, find the principal normal vector $N(t)$ at an arbitrary point on the curve.\n\n$N(t)=⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$\n{{field-1}}$,0\\,⟩$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068383/state.json" "file_sha256" "dca4d73da748321ea9be4275fec60a4462e60b0acef5a844f0115020b2c0aef3"} {"question" "q-358782" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the unit tangent vector $T(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(12t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,12⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(12)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)+144} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25(1)+144} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{169} \\\\  & =13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit tangent vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}⟨5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,12⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{5}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\frac{5}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\frac{12}{13}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal normal $N(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{5}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\frac{5}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{12}{13})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{5}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-\\frac{5}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{13}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{5}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{(-\\frac{5}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{25}{169}({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{13}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{-\\frac{5}{13}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,0⟩}{(\\frac{5}{13})}=⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068383/state.json" "file_sha256" "dca4d73da748321ea9be4275fec60a4462e60b0acef5a844f0115020b2c0aef3"} {"question" "q-358782" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068383/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "54539cf7dfb885572033a2bf0861ba4715d20e2968cedb79235051206f0c8ae8"} {"question" "q-358782" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068383/state.json" "file_sha256" "dca4d73da748321ea9be4275fec60a4462e60b0acef5a844f0115020b2c0aef3"} {"question" "q-358782" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\cos(t)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068383/state.json" "file_sha256" "dca4d73da748321ea9be4275fec60a4462e60b0acef5a844f0115020b2c0aef3"} {"question" "q-223107" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the curve $r(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+2\\sqrt{2}\\,t\\,k$, find the principal normal vector $N(t)$ at an arbitrary point on the curve.\n\n$N(t)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$i\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$\n\n$j$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068383/state.json" "file_sha256" "dca4d73da748321ea9be4275fec60a4462e60b0acef5a844f0115020b2c0aef3"} {"question" "q-223107" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the unit tangent vector $T(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\sqrt{2}\\,t)\\,k \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+2\\sqrt{2}\\,k \\\\  \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(2\\sqrt{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t+8} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit tangent vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+2\\sqrt{2}\\,k}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{2}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal normal $N(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{1}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i-\\frac{1}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{2})\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+0\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\  \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{(-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{9}({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j}{(\\frac{1}{3})} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068383/state.json" "file_sha256" "dca4d73da748321ea9be4275fec60a4462e60b0acef5a844f0115020b2c0aef3"} {"question" "q-223107" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068383/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "54539cf7dfb885572033a2bf0861ba4715d20e2968cedb79235051206f0c8ae8"} {"question" "q-223107" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068383/state.json" "file_sha256" "dca4d73da748321ea9be4275fec60a4462e60b0acef5a844f0115020b2c0aef3"} {"question" "q-223107" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\sin(t)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068383/state.json" "file_sha256" "dca4d73da748321ea9be4275fec60a4462e60b0acef5a844f0115020b2c0aef3"} {"question" "q-222851" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given  the curve $r(t)=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,4t⟩$, find the principal normal vector $N(t)$ at an arbitrary point on the curve.\n\n$N(t)=⟨\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,$\n{{field-1}}$,0\\,⟩$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068383/state.json" "file_sha256" "dca4d73da748321ea9be4275fec60a4462e60b0acef5a844f0115020b2c0aef3"} {"question" "q-222851" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the unit tangent vector $T(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit tangent vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\frac{4}{5}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal normal $N(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{4}{5})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{5}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{(-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{25}({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{-\\frac{3}{5}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0⟩}{(\\frac{3}{5})}=⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068383/state.json" "file_sha256" "dca4d73da748321ea9be4275fec60a4462e60b0acef5a844f0115020b2c0aef3"} {"question" "q-222851" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068383/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "54539cf7dfb885572033a2bf0861ba4715d20e2968cedb79235051206f0c8ae8"} {"question" "q-222851" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068383/state.json" "file_sha256" "dca4d73da748321ea9be4275fec60a4462e60b0acef5a844f0115020b2c0aef3"} {"question" "q-222851" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\sin(t)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068383/state.json" "file_sha256" "dca4d73da748321ea9be4275fec60a4462e60b0acef5a844f0115020b2c0aef3"} {"question" "q-223101" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nT(t)=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)\\,i+\\sqrt{3}\\,j+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1)\\,k).\n$$\n\nThe unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given above. Find the principal normal vector to that curve at an arbitrary point on the curve.\n\n$N(t)=$\n$\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1)\\,i\\,-$\n{{field-1}}\n$k$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068383/state.json" "file_sha256" "dca4d73da748321ea9be4275fec60a4462e60b0acef5a844f0115020b2c0aef3"} {"question" "q-223101" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)\\,i+\\sqrt{3}\\,j+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1)\\,k)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1))\\,i+\\sqrt{3}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1))\\,k) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1)\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)\\,k)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\frac{1}{2}‖\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1)\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)\\,k‖ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1))^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡(t+1)+{sin}^{2}⁡(t+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1)\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)\\,k)}{(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1)\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068383/state.json" "file_sha256" "dca4d73da748321ea9be4275fec60a4462e60b0acef5a844f0115020b2c0aef3"} {"question" "q-223101" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068383/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "54539cf7dfb885572033a2bf0861ba4715d20e2968cedb79235051206f0c8ae8"} {"question" "q-223101" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068383/state.json" "file_sha256" "dca4d73da748321ea9be4275fec60a4462e60b0acef5a844f0115020b2c0aef3"} {"question" "q-223101" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sin(t+1)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068383/state.json" "file_sha256" "dca4d73da748321ea9be4275fec60a4462e60b0acef5a844f0115020b2c0aef3"} {"question" "q-127577" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+1}\\cdot q(t).\n$$\n\nFind the principal normal vector at the point where $t=1$, if \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(1)=-i+j+\\sqrt{2}\\,k,\\,{q}^{′}(1)=2\\,j+\\sqrt{2}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068383/state.json" "file_sha256" "dca4d73da748321ea9be4275fec60a4462e60b0acef5a844f0115020b2c0aef3"} {"question" "q-127577" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+1}\\cdot q(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+1})\\cdot q(t)+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+1}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(q(t)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2t}{({t}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\cdot q(t)+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+1}\\cdot {q}^{′}(t) \\\\  \\\\ {T}^{′}(1) & =-\\frac{2(1)}{({1}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\cdot q(1)+\\frac{1}{{1}^{2}+1}\\cdot {q}^{′}(1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(-i+j+\\sqrt{2}\\,k)+\\frac{1}{2}(2\\,j+\\sqrt{2}\\,k) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(i+j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(1)‖ & =|\\frac{1}{2}|\\sqrt{(1)^{2}+(1)^{2}+(0)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(1)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve at the point where $t=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(1) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(1)}{‖{T}^{′}(1)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}(i+j)}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(i+j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that when we find the scalar product of $N$ and $T$ at the point where $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(1)\\cdot N(1) & =\\frac{1}{2}q(1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(i+j) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-i+j+\\sqrt{2}\\,k)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(i+j) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}(-1+1+0) \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068383/state.json" "file_sha256" "dca4d73da748321ea9be4275fec60a4462e60b0acef5a844f0115020b2c0aef3"} {"question" "q-127577" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068383/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "54539cf7dfb885572033a2bf0861ba4715d20e2968cedb79235051206f0c8ae8"} {"question" "q-127577" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068383/state.json" "file_sha256" "dca4d73da748321ea9be4275fec60a4462e60b0acef5a844f0115020b2c0aef3"} {"question" "q-127577" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{1}{3}(2\\,i-j+2\\,k)"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{6}(-2\\,j+\\sqrt{2}\\,k)"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(i+j)"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(i-k)"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}(i+j-2\\,k)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068383/state.json" "file_sha256" "dca4d73da748321ea9be4275fec60a4462e60b0acef5a844f0115020b2c0aef3"} {"question" "q-127577" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(i+j)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068383/state.json" "file_sha256" "dca4d73da748321ea9be4275fec60a4462e60b0acef5a844f0115020b2c0aef3"} {"question" "q-127598" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{1}{{e}^{t}+1}\\cdot q(t).\n$$\n\nFind the principal normal vector at the point where $t=0,$ if \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(0)=⟨1,\\sqrt{2},1⟩,\\,{q}^{′}(0)=⟨1,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068383/state.json" "file_sha256" "dca4d73da748321ea9be4275fec60a4462e60b0acef5a844f0115020b2c0aef3"} {"question" "q-127598" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{{e}^{t}+1}\\cdot q(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{{e}^{t}+1})\\cdot q(t)+\\frac{1}{{e}^{t}+1}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(q(t)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{t}}{({e}^{t}+1)^{2}}\\cdot q(t)+\\frac{1}{{e}^{t}+1}\\cdot {q}^{′}(t) \\\\  \\\\ {T}^{′}(0) & =-\\frac{{e}^{0}}{({e}^{0}+1)^{2}}\\cdot q(0)+\\frac{1}{{e}^{0}+1}\\cdot {q}^{′}(0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}q(0)+\\frac{1}{2}{q}^{′}(0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}⟨1,\\sqrt{2},1⟩+\\frac{1}{2}⟨1,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(-⟨1,\\sqrt{2},1⟩+2⟨1,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},0⟩) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}⟨1,0,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(0)‖ & =\\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(0)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve at the point where $t=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(0) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(0)}{‖{T}^{′}(0)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{4}⟨1,0,-1⟩}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,0,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that when we find the scalar product of $T$ and $N$ at the point where $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(0)\\cdot N(0) & =\\frac{1}{2}q(0)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨1,\\sqrt{2},1⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}(1(1)+\\sqrt{2}(0)+1(-1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}(1+0-1) \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068383/state.json" "file_sha256" "dca4d73da748321ea9be4275fec60a4462e60b0acef5a844f0115020b2c0aef3"} {"question" "q-127598" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068383/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "54539cf7dfb885572033a2bf0861ba4715d20e2968cedb79235051206f0c8ae8"} {"question" "q-127598" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068383/state.json" "file_sha256" "dca4d73da748321ea9be4275fec60a4462e60b0acef5a844f0115020b2c0aef3"} {"question" "q-127598" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{1}{2}⟨-1,\\sqrt{2},-1⟩"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{11}}⟨2\\sqrt{2},-1,-\\sqrt{2}⟩"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,0,-1⟩"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨-\\sqrt{2},1,0⟩"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨1,0,\\sqrt{2}⟩"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068383/state.json" "file_sha256" "dca4d73da748321ea9be4275fec60a4462e60b0acef5a844f0115020b2c0aef3"} {"question" "q-127598" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,0,-1⟩"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068383/state.json" "file_sha256" "dca4d73da748321ea9be4275fec60a4462e60b0acef5a844f0115020b2c0aef3"} {"question" "q-127595" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+4t}}\\cdot q(t).\n$$\n\nFind the principal normal vector at the point where $t=2,$ if \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(2)=⟨1,2\\sqrt{2}⟩,\\,{q}^{′}(2)=⟨0,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068383/state.json" "file_sha256" "dca4d73da748321ea9be4275fec60a4462e60b0acef5a844f0115020b2c0aef3"} {"question" "q-127595" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+4t}}\\cdot q(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+4t}})\\cdot q(t)+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+4t}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(q(t)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{(1+4t)^{3/2}}\\cdot q(t)+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+4t}}\\cdot {q}^{′}(t) \\\\  \\\\ {T}^{′}(2) & =-\\frac{2}{(1+4(2))^{3/2}}\\cdot q(2)+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+4(2)}}\\cdot {q}^{′}(2) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{27}⟨1,2\\sqrt{2}⟩+\\frac{1}{3}⟨0,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{27}(-2⟨1,2\\sqrt{2}⟩+9⟨0,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟩) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{27}⟨-2,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{54}⟨-4,\\sqrt{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(2)‖ & =\\frac{1}{54}\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}+(\\sqrt{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{54}\\cdot \\sqrt{18} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{18}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(2)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve at the point where $t=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(2) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(2)}{‖{T}^{′}(2)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{54}⟨-4,\\sqrt{2}⟩}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{18})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}⟨-4,\\sqrt{2}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨-2\\sqrt{2},1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that when we find the scalar product of $T$ and $N$ at the point where $t=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(2)\\cdot N(2) & =\\frac{1}{3}q(2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}⟨-2\\sqrt{2},1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨1,2\\sqrt{2}⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}⟨-2\\sqrt{2},1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}(1\\cdot (-2\\sqrt{2})+2\\sqrt{2}\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}(-2\\sqrt{2}+2\\sqrt{2}) \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068383/state.json" "file_sha256" "dca4d73da748321ea9be4275fec60a4462e60b0acef5a844f0115020b2c0aef3"} {"question" "q-127595" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068383/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "54539cf7dfb885572033a2bf0861ba4715d20e2968cedb79235051206f0c8ae8"} {"question" "q-127595" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068383/state.json" "file_sha256" "dca4d73da748321ea9be4275fec60a4462e60b0acef5a844f0115020b2c0aef3"} {"question" "q-127595" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{1}{3}⟨-1,2\\sqrt{2}⟩"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{3}⟨-2\\sqrt{2},1⟩"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨-1,\\sqrt{2}⟩"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨-2,\\sqrt{2}⟩"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨2,-\\sqrt{2}⟩"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068383/state.json" "file_sha256" "dca4d73da748321ea9be4275fec60a4462e60b0acef5a844f0115020b2c0aef3"} {"question" "q-127595" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{3}⟨-2\\sqrt{2},1⟩"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068383/state.json" "file_sha256" "dca4d73da748321ea9be4275fec60a4462e60b0acef5a844f0115020b2c0aef3"} {"question" "q-127600" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+2}\\cdot q(t).\n$$\n\nFind the principal normal vector at the point where $t=0$, if \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(0)=\\sqrt{2}\\,i+j+k,\\,{q}^{′}(0)=-2\\,j+2\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068383/state.json" "file_sha256" "dca4d73da748321ea9be4275fec60a4462e60b0acef5a844f0115020b2c0aef3"} {"question" "q-127600" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+2}\\cdot q(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+2})\\cdot q(t)+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(q(t)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2t}{({t}^{2}+2)^{2}}\\cdot q(t)+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+2}\\cdot {q}^{′}(t) \\\\  \\\\ {T}^{′}(0) & =-\\frac{2(0)}{({0}^{2}+2)^{2}}\\cdot q(0)+\\frac{1}{{0}^{2}+2}\\cdot {q}^{′}(0) \\\\  & =0(\\sqrt{2}\\,i+j+k)+\\frac{1}{2}(-2\\,j+2\\,k) \\\\  & =-j+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(0)‖ & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{(-1)}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(0)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve at the point where $t=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(0) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(0)}{‖{T}^{′}(0)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{-j+k}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(-j+k)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that when we find the scalar product of $T$ and $N$ at the point where $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(0)\\cdot N(0) & =\\frac{1}{2}q(0)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(-j+k) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{2}\\,i+j+k)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(-j+k) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}(-j\\cdot j+k\\cdot k) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}(-1+1) \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068383/state.json" "file_sha256" "dca4d73da748321ea9be4275fec60a4462e60b0acef5a844f0115020b2c0aef3"} {"question" "q-127600" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068383/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "54539cf7dfb885572033a2bf0861ba4715d20e2968cedb79235051206f0c8ae8"} {"question" "q-127600" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068383/state.json" "file_sha256" "dca4d73da748321ea9be4275fec60a4462e60b0acef5a844f0115020b2c0aef3"} {"question" "q-127600" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{1}{2}(-\\sqrt{2}\\,i+\\,j+k)"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{3}(-2\\,i+\\,j+2\\,k)"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{3}(2\\,i-\\,j+2\\,k)"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(-j+k)"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(\\sqrt{2}\\,i-2\\,k)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068383/state.json" "file_sha256" "dca4d73da748321ea9be4275fec60a4462e60b0acef5a844f0115020b2c0aef3"} {"question" "q-127600" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(-j+k)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068383/state.json" "file_sha256" "dca4d73da748321ea9be4275fec60a4462e60b0acef5a844f0115020b2c0aef3"} {"question" "q-75491" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{5}\\cdot q(t).\n$$\n\nFind the principal normal vector at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ if \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=⟨3,0,-4⟩,\\,{q}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=⟨0,3,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068383/state.json" "file_sha256" "dca4d73da748321ea9be4275fec60a4462e60b0acef5a844f0115020b2c0aef3"} {"question" "q-75491" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{5}\\cdot q(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{5})\\cdot q(t)+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{5}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(q(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{5}\\cdot q(t)+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{5}\\cdot {q}^{′}(t) \\\\  \\\\ {T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{5}\\cdot q(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{5}\\cdot {q}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{q}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨0,3,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}⟨0,1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})‖ & =\\frac{3}{5}\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}}=\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{‖{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{3}{5}⟨0,1,0⟩}{(\\frac{3}{5})} \\\\  & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that when we find the scalar product of $T$ and $N$ at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot N(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\frac{1}{5}q(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot ⟨0,1,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨3,0,-4⟩\\cdot ⟨0,1,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(3\\cdot 0+0\\cdot 1-4\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068383/state.json" "file_sha256" "dca4d73da748321ea9be4275fec60a4462e60b0acef5a844f0115020b2c0aef3"} {"question" "q-75491" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068383/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "54539cf7dfb885572033a2bf0861ba4715d20e2968cedb79235051206f0c8ae8"} {"question" "q-75491" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068383/state.json" "file_sha256" "dca4d73da748321ea9be4275fec60a4462e60b0acef5a844f0115020b2c0aef3"} {"question" "q-75491" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{1}{5}⟨4,0,3⟩"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨0,-1,1⟩"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}⟨-1,1,-1⟩"] ["math" "⟨0,0,-1⟩"] ["math" "⟨0,1,0⟩"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068383/state.json" "file_sha256" "dca4d73da748321ea9be4275fec60a4462e60b0acef5a844f0115020b2c0aef3"} {"question" "q-75491" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "⟨0,1,0⟩"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068383/state.json" "file_sha256" "dca4d73da748321ea9be4275fec60a4462e60b0acef5a844f0115020b2c0aef3"} {"question" "e-14996" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf T(t) =\\sin{t} \\,\\mathbf i -\\cos{t}\\,\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nFind the principal normal vector at the point where $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068383/example-14996.json" "file_sha256" "66e267f5bb80a2a38406eb2c29209c6a084695d5d93add3586d7371d8d042776"} {"question" "e-14996" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Remember that in general, the principal normal vector is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf N(t)= \\dfrac{\\mathbf T'(t)}{\\|\\mathbf T'(t)\\|}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate $\\mathbf T'(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i-(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j \\\\ {T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,i+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i+j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of $\\mathbf T'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖ & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}‖i+j‖ \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\sqrt{{(1)}^{2}+{(1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\sqrt{2} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $\\mathbf T'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)$ and obtain the principal normal vector to the curve at the point where $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}{‖{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖}=\\frac{\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i+j)}{1}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,i+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo check our result, we can verify that $\\mathbf T$ and $\\mathbf N$ are perpendicular by computing their dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\cdot N(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,i-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,i+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068383/example-14996.json" "file_sha256" "66e267f5bb80a2a38406eb2c29209c6a084695d5d93add3586d7371d8d042776"} {"question" "e-14997" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given  the curve $\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle \\sqrt 3 t , \\:  \\cos t, \\: \\sin{t}\\right\\rangle,$ find the principal normal vector $\\mathbf N(t)$ at an arbitrary point on the curve." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068383/example-14997.json" "file_sha256" "c0fe5fd5e51ca09d4656cd92964e56833edbb0a6bb0ab130d0c7124e90ac2a4d"} {"question" "e-14997" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Remember that in general, the principal normal vector is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf N(t)= \\dfrac{\\mathbf T'(t)}{\\|\\mathbf T'(t)\\|}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the unit tangent vector $\\mathbf T(t).$ To do this, we calculate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{3}t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\sqrt{3},\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3+{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit tangent vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨\\sqrt{3},\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\,-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{2},\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal normal $\\mathbf{N}(t).$ To do this, we calculate $\\mathbf T'(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨0,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $\\mathbf T'(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}⟨0,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩}{(\\frac{1}{2})}=⟨0,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo check our result, we can verify that $\\mathbf T$ and $\\mathbf N$ are perpendicular by computing their dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t)\\cdot N(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\,-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{2},\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\cdot 0+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{2}-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{2} \\\\  & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068383/example-14997.json" "file_sha256" "c0fe5fd5e51ca09d4656cd92964e56833edbb0a6bb0ab130d0c7124e90ac2a4d"} {"question" "e-14998" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf T(t) = \\sin{t} \\cdot \\mathbf q(t).\n$$\n\nFind the principal normal vector at the point where $t = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4},$ if \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,i-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,j+k,\\,{q}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=-2\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068383/example-14998.json" "file_sha256" "379181c237f8157e95e5780511ae6b8251ce135d9c50ef51d4608440759f9106"} {"question" "e-14998" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Remember that in general, the principal normal vector is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf N(t)= \\dfrac{\\mathbf T'(t)}{\\|\\mathbf T'(t)\\|}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate $\\mathbf T'(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot q(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot q(t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(q(t)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot q(t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot {q}^{′}(t) \\\\  \\\\ {T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\cdot q(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\cdot {q}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,i-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,j+k)+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(-2\\,k) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\,i-\\frac{1}{2}\\,j-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of $\\mathbf T'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖ & =\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $\\mathbf T'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve at the point where $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}{‖{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\frac{1}{2}\\,i-\\frac{1}{2}\\,j-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,k}{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\,i-\\frac{1}{2}\\,j-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068383/example-14998.json" "file_sha256" "379181c237f8157e95e5780511ae6b8251ce135d9c50ef51d4608440759f9106"} {"question" "q-222851" "field" nil "attribute" "question/answer-fields" "value" [{"key" "field-1" "type"