 basis 2308. The saved live DOM and before screenshot supply N_x=-cos(t) and N_z=0 and contain exactly one blank, field-1, representing N_y. The after screenshot and successful grade confirm the entered -sin(t). Differentiating T(t)=<-3/5 sin(t),3/5 cos(t),4/5> gives T′(t)=<-3/5 cos(t),-3/5 sin(t),0>; normalizing gives N(t)=<-cos(t),-sin(t),0>, matching the authentic worked solution. Replace the complete current ownership layout with one fresh blank identity using only its live -sin(t) answer; retain every original component, UUID and historical prompt unchanged. This is a presentation-layout replacement, with no mathematical correction to the original source."}] "usage" {"e-14998" [] "q-222851" [] "e-14997" [] "q-33233" [] "e-14996" []} "basis" 2312 "retractions" [[17592186120175 :question/problem "The equations of a curve $r(t)$ and its corresponding unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at an arbitrary point are given below. Find the principal normal vector to that curve at the point $P(0, 3, 4)$.\n\n$[MATH: r(t)|= 5\\cos ti + 3\\sin tj + 4\\sin tk, t ∈ [0, 2π); T(t)|=-\\sin ti + \\frac{3}{5}\\cos tj + \\frac{4}{5}\\cos tk, t ∈ [0, 2π)]$"] [17592186120089 :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t) = ⟨3\\cos t, 3\\sin t, 4t⟩$, find the principal normal vector $N(t)$ at an arbitrary point on the curve.$N(t) =$\n$⟨$\n{{field-1}}\n$$,\n{{field-2}}\n$$,\n{{field-3}}\n$⟩$\n\n---\n\n<a id=\"deriving-the-formula-for-the-principal-normal\"></a>"] [17592186120089 :question/answer-fields 17592186117080] [17592186120089 :question/answer-fields 17592186117081] [17592186120089 :question/answer-fields 17592186117082] [17592186119455 :question/problem "The unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given by\n$\\mathbf T(t) =\\sin{t} \\,\\mathbf i -\\cos{t}\\,\\mathbf j$.\nFind the principal normal vector at the point where $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$."] [17592186119455 :question/worked-solution "Remember that in general, the principal normal vector is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf N(t)= \\dfrac{\\mathbf T'(t)}{\\|\\mathbf T'(t)\\|}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate $\\mathbf T'(t)$ and evaluate it at\n\n$$\nt=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT^{′}(t) &= \\frac{d}{dt}(\\sin ti - \\cos tj) \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(\\sin t)i - \\frac{d}{dt}(\\cos t)j \\\\\n&= \\cos ti - (-\\sin t)j \\\\\n&= \\cos ti + \\sin tj \\\\\nT^{′}(\\frac{π}{4}) &= \\cos (\\frac{π}{4})i + \\sin (\\frac{π}{4})j \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}i + \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}j \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i + j)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the magnitude of\n\n$$\n\\mathbf T'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n‖T^{′}(\\frac{π}{4})‖ &= \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}‖i + j‖ \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\sqrt{(1)^{2} + (1)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\sqrt{2} \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize\n\n$$\n\\mathbf T'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)\n$$\n\nand obtain the principal normal vector to the curve at the point where\n\n$$\nt=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nN(\\frac{π}{4}) &= (T^{′}(\\frac{π}{4}))/(‖T^{′}(\\frac{π}{4})‖) = (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i + j))/(1) = \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}i + \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo check our result, we can verify that $\\mathbf T$ and $\\mathbf N$ are perpendicular by computing their dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT(\\frac{π}{4}) \\cdot N(\\frac{π}{4}) &= (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}i - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}j) \\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}i + \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}j) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\n&= 0✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186119456 :question/problem "Given the curve $\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle \\sqrt 3 t, \\: \\cos t, \\: \\sin{t}\\right\\rangle$, find the principal normal vector $\\mathbf N(t)$ at an arbitrary point on the curve."] [17592186119456 :question/worked-solution "Remember that in general, the principal normal vector is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf N(t)= \\dfrac{\\mathbf T'(t)}{\\|\\mathbf T'(t)\\|}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the unit tangent vector $\\mathbf T(t)$. To do this, we calculate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= ⟨\\frac{d}{dt}(\\sqrt{3}t), \\frac{d}{dt}(\\cos t), \\frac{d}{dt}(\\sin t)⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨\\sqrt{3},-\\sin t, \\cos t⟩ \\\\\n‖r^{′}(t)‖ &= \\sqrt{(\\sqrt{3})^{2} + (-\\sin t)^{2} + (\\cos t)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{3 + \\sin^{2} t + \\cos^{2} t} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{4} \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit tangent vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT(t) &= (r^{′}(t))/(‖r^{′}(t)‖) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}⟨\\sqrt{3},-\\sin t, \\cos t⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{\\sin t}{2}, \\frac{\\cos t}{2}⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal normal $\\mathbf{N}(t)$. To do this, we calculate $\\mathbf T'(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT^{′}(t) &= ⟨\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}), \\frac{d}{dt}(-\\frac{\\sin t}{2}), \\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{\\cos t}{2})⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨0,-\\frac{1}{2}\\cos t,-\\frac{1}{2}\\sin t⟩ \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}⟨0,-\\cos t,-\\sin t⟩ \\\\\n‖T^{′}(t)‖ &= \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{0^{2} + (-\\cos t)^{2} + (-\\sin t)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{\\cos^{2} t + \\sin^{2} t} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $\\mathbf T'(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nN(t) &= (T^{′}(t))/(‖T^{′}(t)‖) = (\\frac{1}{2}⟨0,-\\cos t,-\\sin t⟩)/((\\frac{1}{2})) = ⟨0,-\\cos t,-\\sin t⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo check our result, we can verify that $\\mathbf T$ and $\\mathbf N$ are perpendicular by computing their dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT(t) \\cdot N(t) &= ⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{\\sin t}{2}, \\frac{\\cos t}{2}⟩ \\cdot ⟨0,-\\cos t,-\\sin t⟩ \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\cdot 0 + \\frac{\\sin t\\cos t}{2} - \\frac{\\cos t\\sin t}{2} \\\\\n&= 0✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186119457 :question/problem "The unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given by\n$\\mathbf T(t) = \\sin{t} \\cdot \\mathbf q(t)$.\nFind the principal normal vector at the point where $t = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$, if\n$q(\\frac{π}{4}) =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}i - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}j + k, q^{′}(\\frac{π}{4}) =-2k$."] [17592186119457 :question/worked-solution "Remember that in general, the principal normal vector is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf N(t)= \\dfrac{\\mathbf T'(t)}{\\|\\mathbf T'(t)\\|}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate $\\mathbf T'(t)$ and evaluate it at\n\n$$\nt=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT^{′}(t) &= \\frac{d}{dt}(\\sin t \\cdot q(t)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(\\sin t) \\cdot q(t) + \\sin t \\cdot \\frac{d}{dt}(q(t)) \\\\\n&= \\cos t \\cdot q(t) + \\sin t \\cdot q^{′}(t) \\\\\nT^{′}(\\frac{π}{4}) &= \\cos (\\frac{π}{4}) \\cdot q(\\frac{π}{4}) + \\sin (\\frac{π}{4}) \\cdot q^{′}(\\frac{π}{4}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}i - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}j + k) + \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(-2k) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{2}i - \\frac{1}{2}j - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the magnitude of\n\n$$\n\\mathbf T'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n‖T^{′}(\\frac{π}{4})‖ &= \\sqrt{(-\\frac{1}{2})^{2} + (-\\frac{1}{2})^{2} + (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4} + \\frac{1}{4} + \\frac{1}{2}} \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize\n\n$$\n\\mathbf T'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)\n$$\n\nand obtain the principal normal to the curve at the point where\n\n$$\nt=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nN(\\frac{π}{4}) &= (T^{′}(\\frac{π}{4}))/(‖T^{′}(\\frac{π}{4})‖) \\\\\n&= \\frac{-\\frac{1}{2}i - \\frac{1}{2}j - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}k}{1} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{2}i - \\frac{1}{2}j - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186117571 17592186117572 17592186117573 17592186117574 17592186117575] "immutable_fields" [[17592186117080 :answer-field/id] [17592186117080 :answer-field/key] [17592186117080 :answer-field/type] [17592186117081 :answer-field/id] [17592186117081 :answer-field/key] [1759218611708